# seal -- this repo's Tilt extension: generic project config,
# credential-to-Secret resolution, and CI test-result reporting. The one
# extension a project registers; everything else it needs, including
# /tilt/docker_build's cached_docker_build(), seal reaches for itself
# (see /tilt/README.md at the repo root).
#
# This file wires together the internal Tiltfiles below (the logic itself
# lives in config.Tiltfile / resources.Tiltfile /
# tests.Tiltfile / build.Tiltfile / outcomes.Tiltfile -- see README.md for
# what each does) and re-exports only what a project's own Tiltfile needs to
# call directly: select_k8s_overlay(), seal_service(), reset_resource_name(),
# tests_run_resource_name(), register_outcome_runner(), and
# k8s_overlay/publish_images (needed for the odd bit of project-level
# branching outside seal_service() -- see examples/angular-django/Tiltfile).
# Everything else those files define (register_syncback(),
# seal_resource_name(), PROJECT_ROOT, build_type) is wired
# together between them directly
# (see their own load() statements) and never needed from outside seal
# itself.
#
# Deliberately load_dynamic(), not load(): Starlark only treats a name as
# part of *this* module's own exportable globals if it's explicitly defined
# here -- a name merely re-imported via a `load()` statement doesn't count,
# even if re-bound to itself, so `load('ext://seal', 'k8s_overlay')`
# from a caller fails with "name k8s_overlay not found in module
# ext://seal" if plain load() is used here instead.
# load_dynamic() sidesteps that: it returns a plain dict, so pulling a name
# out of it is a genuine expression, making the resulting global a real,
# re-exportable definition of this module's own.
_config = load_dynamic('./config.Tiltfile')
k8s_overlay = _config['k8s_overlay']
publish_images = _config['publish_images']
select_k8s_overlay = _config['select_k8s_overlay']

# build.Tiltfile composes config.Tiltfile/tests.Tiltfile and
# /tilt/docker_build's cached_docker_build() into one call -- see its own
# docstring.
_build = load_dynamic('./build.Tiltfile')
seal_service = _build['seal_service']

# Re-exported so a project can name a service's reset resource without
# hardcoding the 'seal_reset_<name>' spelling -- what an end-to-end test
# resource puts in its own resource_deps (see build.Tiltfile).
reset_resource_name = _build['reset_resource_name']

# Same, for the resource that runs a service's own tests inside its running
# container -- what anything that must not overlap with that run names. Only
# a service that declared a `test_command` has one. Taken from
# tests.Tiltfile, which build.Tiltfile has already load()ed by now, so this
# reaches the same module rather than evaluating a second copy of it.
_tests = load_dynamic('./tests.Tiltfile')
tests_run_resource_name = _tests['tests_run_resource_name']

# The project's whole outcome tree, wired in by one call from its root
# Tiltfile -- see outcomes.Tiltfile and /rfcs/0010-outcome-tree.md.
_outcomes = load_dynamic('./outcomes.Tiltfile')
register_outcome_runner = _outcomes['register_outcome_runner']
